FORMULA Tutorial
=================
for: PSION System 3a/c/Siena

Author: (C) Peter von Walter; 26.5.99
email: vwalter@physi.uni-heidelberg.de
www: http://pi.physi.uni-heidelberg.de/~vwalter/my/homepage.html

Installation
------------
Please read FORMULA.TXT and do the installation!


Start the program
-----------------
- Select the file "UNITS" under the FORMULA icon and open it with ENTER.
A (nag) dialog with information about the program and how long the DEMO will be valid is shown. You
could go with ENTER to the registration but for now..

- Press ESC to accept the demo mode.
The program starts with an empty entry field (below) and a log area above.


Opening records
---------------
- Enter 'inch' and press ENTER to search for the data record with the name 'inch'. With the provided
example file 'UNITS' it will find and open a dialog which shows the basic conversions as stated in
the record. This record is actually written for unit conversion (see later) but will be presented
here as a table and allows you to enter any value in any line for recalculation. 

- Press ESC to exit the dialog mode.

- Try again with some other records like 'ounce'. Note also the different domains (us, imperial,
apothecaris, troy, ..). Experiment with the menu 'Record' and the items 'Group' and 'Select' to get
an overview of your database for conversions and which help you to enter the unit names. Experiment
with the powerful 'Find' function. Always press ENTER after selecting!


- Select from the Menu 'File' the item 'DATA'. This immediately opens the current data base UNITS.DBF
with the DATA application and allows you to inspect, to edit or to add and delete records at your
will. But use it now only for a short browse through the data base! A good practice might be to look
for the data record 'inch', previously opened, and to see how it really looks like!

- Close the DATA application and reenter FORMULA with PSION-X. IMPORTANT: Always exit the DATA
application first, before entering another program!


Simple conversions
---------------------
- Enter 'inch=' (and press ENTER). This is the entry of an equation and does not open a dialog but
tries to solve the equation. As you did not enter the target unit you ask implicetely for the
standard units (SI), in this case 'meter' and this gives the equivalence of '0.0254 m'. The completed
equation will be recorded in the log area.

- Enter '3 inch=' and the result will be accordingly '0.0762 m'.

- Now enter '3 inch=cm'. As you ask explicetely for the equivalence in 'cm', the system will
calculate '7.62 cm'. Got the idea!?

- Try some more, like '3 foot=inch' or 'lightyear=foot'!!


Complex conversions
-------------------
- Enter '2 yard2=cm2'; Answer='16722.5..cm2'. This shows how the program handles derived units like
areas and the prefixes 'c'='centi'=1/100. Note, that derived units do not have to be in the database,
they always will be calculated by the program itself from the basic units.

- Enter '10 yard/sec='; Answer='9.144 m s-1', meaning SI units 'meter per second'.

- Now enter '10 yard/sec=mile. Obviously both sides of the equation are NOT compatible, You can not
convert a speed to a length. In this case the program always tries to solve the equation by
complementing the missing units. Therefore the answer is '0.0056.. mile s-1'. Simply add '=mile/h'
(or '=mph') in the entry field to get the more appropriate result.

- Try any expressions on both sides of the equation. Note how the program searches through the
database for resolving dependent units (which could always take a couple of seconds).


Builtin functions and constants
-------------------------------
You can use all standard functions and constants which are also available in the calculator:

- Enter 'sin(pi/2)'; Answer='1'.

Any additional constants can be defined in the database (e.g. 'electron').


Adding units
------------
Study the following examples and you will get rules:

- Enter '1 yard + 1 cm'; Answer='1.0109.. yard'.
- Enter '1 cm + 1 yard'; Answer='92.44 cm'. Automatically convert to the first unit!
- Enter '1 cm + 1 yard='; Answer='0.9244 m'. Convert to standard units (SI)!
- Enter '1 cm + 1 yard=inch'; Answer='36.4 inch'. Is this what you wanted?

This new feature allows you to add/subtract different units from each other. Of course you only can
add/subtract units of the same dimension. It does not make sense to add 'inch' to 'sec', does it?!


Time and Date functions
-----------------------
See the following examples (Date format='day.month.year'; Time format='24hour:minute:second'):

- Enter '1.2.99'+1000d'; Answer='28.10.2001'.
You have added 1000 days (of course Y2K compliant!) to the date 1.Feb.1999.

- Enter '1.2.99 10:00-60s';Answer='1.2.1999 9:59:0'.
You subtracted 60 seconds from a date and time!

- Enter '..'; Answer= e.g. '26.5.1999'.
If you omit any information, the program fills it with the current date information.

- Enter '::'; Answer= e.g. '26.5.1999 11:30:52'.
This is the current date and time.

Note: If you convert to a date you are limited to the date between 1.1.1970 and 1.1.2100.


Using the Find function
-----------------------
The Menu function 'Find' is very simple to use but powerful. Here are some rules:

- Press Psion+f for invoking the Find dialog.

- Enter String:'inch' and select Field:Name.
This will search for the record with the exact match in name with 'inch'. Actually there is none,
because the name of the record 'inch' also contains alias names. The program will then add
automatically wildcards to the end (and then to the beginning) of the string to search for
substrings. It builds up an according list of all matching records. If there is only one, the name of
the record will be immediately copied to the entry field. Press Enter to open the found record.

- Enter String:'*inch*' and select Field:<ALL>.
The search will come up with a list of all records, which contain the substring 'inch' in any field.

The find function will be automatically invoked if your entry into the entry field will not succeed
in the first run. 

After selecting the name of the record will be copied into the current cursor position in the entry
field. Use Enter to evaluate afterwards.


Plotting a function
-------------------
- Enter 'y=x**2;x=-10..10'.
This means, you would like to plot the function y=x**2 in the range from x=-10 to 10. As you did not
define the value range for y, the program evaluates the range automatically (Autorange) and finally
plots the function. Note, that the program filled in the range for y in the entry field!

- Enter 'Value=k**2+1;value=-10..10';0..10'.
in this case you explicitely determined the value range for the ordinate. The function will be
plotted accordingly. 'Value' and 'k' are just labels and will be printed on the chart.

- Enter 'Value=k**2+3;value=-10..10';0..10';overlay
As the x and y ranges are the same you can plot the new function into the same chart as an overlay.

- Press the diamond key to switch between the plot sheet (which will be preserved) and the standard
log area.


Evaluating a table
------------------
- Select and open the file TABLES(.DBF) from the Menu item 'File/Open'.

- Enter 'Times' for a table of different times, which correspond to each other.

- Go to any line and enter a new value for that variable (e.g. 7 days, then press ENTER.
See the corresponding values for all other lines!

- See that you can also enter any expression (e.g. 1/12 year, should be one month!)

- Press ESC to leave the current table.

- Try some of the other tables in the file and browse with DATA through the file to see how the
records are constructed.


Evaluating an equation
----------------------

- Select and open the file EQUATION(.DBF) from the Menu item 'File/Open'.

- Enter 'Cone' for an equation with different variables.

This record 'Cone' describes the known formula V = 1/3 * r**2 * pi *h for the volume of a cone.
Additionally the formulas for coat surface and total surface areas are given.

- Enter 2 for height and 3 for radius and press ENTER (leave the cursor in line radius!).
You will be presented with a new dialog 'Evaluate', which allows you to select either Volume or
height to be calculated. As the program only remembers the last entry (radius) two choices are left.

- Select 'Volume' with ENTER (ENTER).
Radius and Height are used to calculate Volume. M and O are also recalculated!


Plotting an equation
--------------------

- Enter a range '1..10' for radius and press ENTER (leave the cursor in line radius!).
A new dialog 'Plot' opens, which allows to choose one of the four (from r) dependent equations.
Note that also M and O can be selected now.

- Select 'M Surface' and press ENTER for a new plot.
After a short time for evaluating the range of M (autoranging), the function will be plotted.
Note the ranging and labeling of the graph.

- Press any key to return back to the record display.
Watch the entered range for the calculated variable M! You could change this manually and plot again
to produce a graph exactly according to your needs.

- Enter now 10 into line 'h height'.
You are now about to produce the same plot with another (fixed third) parameter.

- IMPORTANT: Select again the range in line radius and press ENTER there!
With this you select what variable will be on the x-Axis!

- Select 'M Surface' again for the plot (default) and press the DIAMOND key for an overlay.
See how the new plot keeps the previous scales and the new graph fits in the picture.

- Press any key to return back to the record display.

- Press ESC until you have been stepped back complete to the main screen.
This will be now the picture you have generated!

- Press 'Psion-C' to clear the picture.


Now its your turn
-----------------
This concludes the tutorial.

You should now have a good impression, what the program could do for you.
Try the other functions for yourself and use the online help in each dialog to find out what they are
for.

Hope to hear from you!
